                <pb facs="0404" n="294" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg294"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2297: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-22T14:00:00+01:00">October 22, 1931—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:55 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>241. My 240, October 22, 9 a.m. I hear that the Japanese position is unfavorable
                    on the crucial “fifth point”, that is, they insist upon their demand for
                    settlement of certain questions connected with the general solution of permanent
                    problems in Manchuria in advance of Japanese evacuation. At its public meeting
                    called for this afternoon the Council will, I understand, fall back upon the
                    resolution which was quoted in my 213, October 17, midnight; 215, October 18, 2
                    p.m.; and 222, October 19, 10 a.m., with certain further alterations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
